  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 136 PM CDT Sat Aug 8 2026\n\nThe primary concern heading into next week continues to be \npotentially dangerous heat, at least through Wednesday.  The \neastern edge of the upper level ridge remains over the eastern \nPlains and Mid Mississippi Valley through the first half of the \nweek. Deterministic guidance keeps the low level thermal ridge over \nMissouri and the southern 1/2 of Illinois through Wednesday with \n850mb temperatures in the 22-25C range.  This agrees well with the \nLREF mean through midweek, and forecast sounding show mixing at \nleast that high in most locations.  This mixes down to to mid and \nupper 90s for highs, perhaps kissing 100-102 in some locations. The \nNBM's forecast highs have a good handle on this for the most part, \nthough It may be a tad cool in northeast Missouri into west \ncentral Illinois.  With the high humidity in place, HI values are \n100-105+ area-wide at least on Monday and Tuesday.  Confidence in \n100+ HI values decreases Wednesday as a short wave rounds the \neastern edge of the ridge and moves through the Mississippi Valley. \nThe deterministic ECMWF and GFS print out QPF Wednesday afternoon \nbetween 18-00Z as this wave moves through.  Afternoon convection \nwould almost certainly put the kibosh on any heat \nheadlines...however, the timing of that wave and potential \nconvection is very uncertain at this time.  If the wave is slower \nand Wednesday is dry, it will almost certainly be at least as warm \nand humid as Monday and Tuesday.\n\nThe deterministic guidance diverges for the latter half of the week \nwith the GFS digging a strong trough into the Ohio Valley which \nproduces an amplified upper level pattern and pronounced northwest \nflow over the Mid Mississippi Valley.  This pushes a cold front \nthrough Missouri and Illinois, and our area remains on the cool side \nof the boundary through Saturday.  The ECMWF remains more zonal with \na couple of much weaker short waves rippling by Thursday through \nSaturday.  While both solutions show cooler conditions for Thursday \nthrough Saturday, there's a lot of uncertainty in how much cooler it \nwill actually be.  High temperature IQRs in the LREF increase from \naround 5-6 degrees on Wednesday to 8-12 degrees Thursday.  By Friday \nand Saturday the high temperature IQR increases to 10-15 degrees in \nmany locations, and 15+ for some.  The NBM looks to be about the \nmiddle of the temperature range with highs mainly in the low to mid \n80s.  These temperatures are entirely plausible, and I see no reason \nto deviate at this time given the uncertainties.",
